# LF everywhere -- in the index and in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# Without `eol=lf`, a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true rewrites text files
# to CRLF on checkout. That fights an editor configured for LF, and it makes
# `cargo fmt --check` depend on the machine it runs on rather than on the code.
# Setting it here rather than in local git config means CI and every clone get
# the same answer. Same line, same reasoning, as `odf-crypto/.gitattributes`.
#
# This repo had a concrete instance of the problem: the tree was 21 files LF
# and 17 CRLF, because tooling that rewrote a file in text mode substituted the
# platform ending on the way out. Anchored edits then failed to match text that
# looked identical on screen.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The fixtures are CFB containers and ZIP archives the suite compares byte for
# byte -- SHA-256 goldens in tests/real_office_fixtures.rs, and the DataSpaces
# blob diff in src/dataspaces_tests.rs. Git auto-detects them as binary, but say
# so outright: one CRLF substitution inside a compressed member corrupts a
# fixture and surfaces as a decryption failure that reads like a crypto bug
# rather than a checkout artifact.
#
# Every extension in tests/fixtures/, named individually rather than by a
# directory glob, so a fixture added at a new path is still covered.
*.doc  binary
*.docx binary
*.xls  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.ppt  binary
*.pptx binary
